# Round 117 — can family / thin-family methods say anything new about ζ itself? (scouts, own knowledge)

All eight ideas: long shot or dead.
* ζ_K = ζ·L(χ_D) averaged over D: a family bound beating X^1 would force ζ zero-free, but every family density
  (ours included) must exclude the trivial character — the large sieve sees no cancellation there. Dead as stated.
* ζ as the trivial member of the ring class family: negligible family weight, no individual control.
* Moments of ζ via ζ·L(χ_D) moments: averaging cannot untwist ζ's own moment problem.
* Deuring–Heilbronn repulsion from an off-line ζ zero: repulsion needs the zero near 1; an off-line zero lies at β ≤ 0.8749. Dead.
* Weil positivity / de Bruijn–Newman / zero-free regions via averaged Dedekind zetas: averages cannot isolate the ζ factor.
* Hilbert–Pólya via ring class spectra: no lift capturing all ζ zeros.
Lesson: thin-family methods give strong results for nontrivial characters only; ζ is the one member they cannot see.
